          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Alexander Solzhenitsyn</strong><br>\nAlexander Solzhenitsyn was born on December 11, 1918, in Kislovodsk, Russia. He grew up during a time of political turmoil and change, which would later influence his writing. Despite the hardships his family faced due to political repression, Solzhenitsyn pursued education and served in the Soviet Army during World War II. His experiences during the war deeply impacted his views on the Soviet government and society.</p>\n\n<p><strong>World War II Service</strong><br>\nSolzhenitsyn served as a commander in the Soviet Red Army during World War II. His service was marked by the chaos and brutality of war, which he witnessed firsthand. In 1945, he was arrested for criticizing Joseph Stalin in private letters to a friend. This event marked the beginning of his long and arduous relationship with the Soviet authorities, which would influence much of his later work.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Imprisonment and Cancer Ward</strong><br>\nAfter his arrest, Solzhenitsyn spent eight years in the Soviet prison system, including time in a labor camp and in exile. These experiences formed the basis for his future novels. During his imprisonment, he was also treated for cancer, which inspired his semi-autobiographical novel \"Cancer Ward,\" exploring the lives of patients in a Soviet hospital and serving as a metaphor for the state of Soviet society.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
