            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Gulag Archipelago</strong><br>\nThe Gulag Archipelago, Solzhenitsyn's most famous work, is a detailed account of the Soviet prison camp system and was compiled from various sources, including his own experiences. This work exposed the cruelty and extent of the Gulag system to the world, challenging the legitimacy of the Soviet Union's moral and political authority.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Exile and Voice of Dissent</strong><br>\nAfter the publication of \"The Gulag Archipelago,\" Solzhenitsyn faced an intense backlash from the Soviet government. In 1974, he was arrested and exiled from the Soviet Union. He settled in the United States, where he became a vocal critic of the Soviet regime, advocating for human rights and freedoms.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nobel Prize in Literature</strong><br>\nIn 1970, Solzhenitsyn was awarded the Nobel Prize in Literature for the ethical force with which he pursued the indispensable traditions of Russian literature. However, he did not attend the award ceremony in Stockholm, fearing that he would not be allowed to return to the Soviet Union if he left.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
