          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Return to Russia</strong><br>\nAfter the collapse of the Soviet Union, Solzhenitsyn returned to Russia in 1994. He continued to write and remained a prominent public figure, often expressing his views on Russian politics and society. His return marked a significant moment in Russian history, symbolizing the end of an era of repression.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Years and Death</strong><br>\nIn his later years, Solzhenitsyn was seen as a moral voice for Russia, despite his sometimes controversial political stances. He continued to influence Russian literature and politics until his death on August 3, 2008. His passing was mourned worldwide, and he was remembered for his contributions to literature and his role in exposing the injustices of the Soviet system.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Influence</strong><br>\nAlexander Solzhenitsyn's legacy is marked by his profound impact on literature and his role in revealing the truth about the Soviet regime. His writings, particularly \"The Gulag Archipelago,\" have influenced not only Russian literature but also the global understanding of totalitarianism. His work remains a testament to the power of the human spirit to resist oppression and to the enduring value of truth and freedom.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
